<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amadou Bamba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1853">1853</a>–<a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ahmadu Bamba, Cheikh Ahmadou Bamba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amadou Bamba was a Senegalese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> teacher and founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mouride-brotherhood">Mouride brotherhood</a>. Preaching work, prayer and non-violent devotion, he founded the holy city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/touba">Touba</a> in 1887 and drew large numbers of followers, which alarmed the French colonial authorities, who exiled him to Gabon from 1895 to 1902 and later to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>. His order became one of the most powerful religious and economic forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amadou-bamba.jpg" alt="Amadou Bamba" width="320" height="507" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AhmaduBamba.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
